1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:03,619
Department administrators use
record definitions to give authors

2
00:00:03,619 --> 00:00:05,690
a consistent form and lifecycle.

3
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:09,520
In Records, open Manage
and choose Definitions.

4
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:13,529
This example builds a department-owned
form for a fictional community

5
00:00:13,529 --> 00:00:14,960
coordination exercise.

6
00:00:15,359 --> 00:00:18,340
The catalog separates
published and draft versions.

7
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:22,020
A definition describes the
form; an individual record

8
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:23,780
stores one completed entry.

9
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:27,360
Choose New definition for
the new exercise, leaving the

10
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,299
product-owned forms unchanged.

11
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:32,140
Start from template is
set to Blank definition.

12
00:00:32,570 --> 00:00:37,349
Give Definition a unique lower-case
key, then enter the Name and Category.

13
00:00:37,719 --> 00:00:42,210
Here the name is Training Only,
Community Software Exercise, and the

14
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:45,059
category is Academy Software Exercises.

15
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:49,190
The key cannot be changed after
creation, so review it before

16
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:50,769
choosing Create definition.

17
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:55,120
The returned editor identifies
version one as Draft and confirms

18
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:56,679
that the definition was created.

19
00:00:57,019 --> 00:00:58,790
It is not yet a published form.

20
00:00:58,989 --> 00:01:03,769
Lifecycle preset is QuickEntry, supporting
a draft followed by finalization.

21
00:01:04,309 --> 00:01:06,639
Records per call is Multiple per call.

22
00:01:07,109 --> 00:01:12,050
Require author attestation at finalize
is checked; reviewer and approver roles

23
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:13,829
are not selected for this example.

24
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:18,800
Under Numbering, Number prefix gives
record numbers a recognizable label.

25
00:01:19,210 --> 00:01:23,060
Sequence width is four and
Assign number is OnFinalize.

26
00:01:23,539 --> 00:01:28,220
Reset yearly is checked, while the station
and incident sequence options are off.

27
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:32,930
These choices provide one annual sequence
for this definition and issue the number

28
00:01:32,930 --> 00:01:36,800
when the record is finalized, rather
than when its draft first appears.

29
00:01:36,989 --> 00:01:38,719
Classification is Standard.

30
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:42,400
In Client surfaces, only
Responder is selected.

31
00:01:42,869 --> 00:01:47,649
Allow offline is off, Allow
attachments is on, and Keep photo

32
00:01:47,649 --> 00:01:49,710
coordinates on attachments is off.

33
00:01:50,159 --> 00:01:53,900
Together, these choices control
where the form is offered, whether

34
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:57,599
it can be used offline, and
whether authors can attach files.

35
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:02,089
The Fields table describes the
unchanged starter form: Summary

36
00:02:02,089 --> 00:02:05,990
is required ShortText, while
Notes is optional LongText.

37
00:02:06,509 --> 00:02:11,029
A short summary helps identify an
entry; longer notes provide its context.

38
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:16,059
Check that table rather than mistaking the
nearby JSON example for the saved form.

39
00:02:16,269 --> 00:02:21,189
At the foot of the reopened draft,
Validate, Save draft and Review impact

40
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:23,200
and publish are separate controls.

41
00:02:23,609 --> 00:02:26,380
Validate checks the definition
without publishing it.

42
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:31,889
The validation result says The definition
is valid, while the version remains Draft.

43
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,979
The save confirmation says Draft saved.

44
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:39,340
That confirms a definition save,
not an individual record entry.

45
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:42,850
Freshly reopening the saved
version retains its numbering

46
00:02:42,889 --> 00:02:44,420
and attestation settings.

47
00:02:44,699 --> 00:02:47,770
Keep the validation result,
saved configuration and

48
00:02:47,770 --> 00:02:49,480
publication state distinct.

49
00:02:49,659 --> 00:02:53,310
Review impact and publish
opens Publish impact.

50
00:02:53,710 --> 00:02:57,440
Before the first publication,
Published version is a dash

51
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:59,320
and Breaking change is No.

52
00:02:59,770 --> 00:03:02,419
Read the impact before
making the version available.

53
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:05,899
Client readiness compares
app availability, client

54
00:03:05,899 --> 00:03:07,509
surfaces and capability.

55
00:03:07,989 --> 00:03:10,179
Only Responder is offered this version.

56
00:03:10,589 --> 00:03:14,880
Review this table before using Publish
to make the version available to authors.

57
00:03:15,069 --> 00:03:17,129
A published version is read-only.

58
00:03:17,639 --> 00:03:20,909
Use Open new draft when the
form needs a later change.

59
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:25,319
History identifies the current published
version and its publication date.

60
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:29,460
Its saved numbering and
author-attestation choices remain visible.

61
00:03:29,899 --> 00:03:34,029
The complete settings view also retains
Standard, the client and attachment

62
00:03:34,029 --> 00:03:36,720
choices, and the Summary and Notes fields.

63
00:03:37,070 --> 00:03:39,390
This is the configuration
authors will use.

64
00:03:39,659 --> 00:03:43,429
The published department form
identifies its name and version one,

65
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:46,089
QuickEntry, above Summary and Notes.

66
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:50,129
Confirm that identity before
typing; a generic Training form

67
00:03:50,129 --> 00:03:51,579
would be a different record type.

68
00:03:52,049 --> 00:03:55,729
The lower form offers Save
draft and Save and finalize.

69
00:03:56,149 --> 00:03:58,559
We start without a file or attestation.

70
00:03:58,970 --> 00:04:03,040
Enter a distinct exercise Summary
and explain its purpose in Notes.

71
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:07,809
Choose Save draft first so the saved
entry can be checked before finalization.

72
00:04:08,189 --> 00:04:12,980
The returned record is Draft, with a draft
reference and no final record number.

73
00:04:13,370 --> 00:04:15,620
Author and Owner are Wallace Boden.

74
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:19,989
Station and group resolves to Command,
although the new form showed None.

75
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:24,750
Read the saved result rather than assuming
that a blank selection remained blank.

76
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:29,360
Keep this reference so later editing and
attachment work stay with the same record.

77
00:04:29,549 --> 00:04:33,359
Use Edit on this draft instead of
opening another New Record form.

78
00:04:33,849 --> 00:04:37,039
Update Notes to explain that the
summary and public handout were

79
00:04:37,039 --> 00:04:39,040
reviewed in the fictional exercise.

80
00:04:39,450 --> 00:04:43,290
Read the complete statement in
the editor, then use Save draft.

81
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:46,540
A fresh readback of the same
reference contains those

82
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:48,620
revised Notes and remains Draft.

83
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,700
The separate readback is the check
that the intended text was retained.

84
00:04:52,939 --> 00:04:57,019
On the saved record, Attachments
provides Choose Files, File

85
00:04:57,019 --> 00:04:59,559
classification and Add attachment.

86
00:04:59,970 --> 00:05:03,959
Choose the public software handout
and select Unrestricted operational

87
00:05:03,959 --> 00:05:06,569
content, then use Add attachment.

88
00:05:06,930 --> 00:05:10,449
This choice is for that file;
it is separate from the form’s

89
00:05:10,449 --> 00:05:11,759
Standard classification.

90
00:05:12,220 --> 00:05:15,289
The fresh list contains the
software handout, with a size

91
00:05:15,289 --> 00:05:17,089
of two hundred forty-two bytes.

92
00:05:17,439 --> 00:05:20,279
The next-upload selector
has reset to Restricted.

93
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:24,999
That reset does not describe the file
already stored; check its retained

94
00:05:24,999 --> 00:05:26,940
metadata in the finalized revision.

95
00:05:27,179 --> 00:05:30,520
Read the saved Summary and Notes
before checking the accuracy

96
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:32,830
attestation and choosing Finalize.

97
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:35,370
The fresh result shows Finalized.

98
00:05:35,739 --> 00:05:41,309
Its number uses our T R N A D M prefix,
the year twenty twenty-six, and the

99
00:05:41,309 --> 00:05:44,179
four-digit sequence zero zero zero one.

100
00:05:44,679 --> 00:05:46,740
The draft reference remains alongside it.

101
00:05:47,179 --> 00:05:50,790
Amend is available for a later
correction to a finalized record.

102
00:05:51,019 --> 00:05:55,470
History now shows revision one,
Finalized, with the author and date.

103
00:05:55,829 --> 00:05:57,300
The handout remains listed.

104
00:05:57,690 --> 00:05:59,609
Use View on that history row.

105
00:06:00,079 --> 00:06:03,630
The resulting HTML revision
identifies the same record.

106
00:06:03,970 --> 00:06:08,300
Its attachment metadata retains
the filename, byte size, checksum

107
00:06:08,539 --> 00:06:10,330
and Unrestricted classification.

108
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:12,769
The checksum matches the public handout.

109
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:17,699
This is the stored classification,
independent of the reset upload selector.

110
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:22,429
Farther down, Values preserves the
saved Summary and Notes alongside the

111
00:06:22,429 --> 00:06:24,200
draft reference and record number.

112
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:28,430
Review this specific revision when
checking what finalization retained.

113
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:31,850
Back in Records, the new
custom row is Finalized.

114
00:06:32,239 --> 00:06:37,419
The three earlier records remain present:
an Amended Training entry, a Work Draft

115
00:06:37,629 --> 00:06:39,869
and the older Finalized Training entry.

116
00:06:40,329 --> 00:06:44,140
Read Record number, Type,
State and Summary together

117
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:45,609
to choose the intended row.

118
00:06:46,010 --> 00:06:50,859
This record-entry sequence created only
its own custom entry; finding it again

119
00:06:50,859 --> 00:06:53,090
does not require creating a replacement.

120
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:56,120
For later use, return to
the definition catalog.

121
00:06:56,600 --> 00:07:00,279
The department-owned row shows
published version one, no draft

122
00:07:00,279 --> 00:07:02,720
version, and its own New Record control.

123
00:07:03,179 --> 00:07:05,489
Use that row to select the intended form.

124
00:07:05,929 --> 00:07:10,320
The actual launcher opens the named
version-one form with Summary and Notes.

125
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:13,120
This later check leaves
the new form empty.

126
00:07:13,499 --> 00:07:17,950
Returning to the queue still shows four
records, with no second custom draft.

127
00:07:18,139 --> 00:07:22,199
Fire and EMS teams can use a
small form for exercise briefings.

128
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:26,269
Community-care and mental-health
teams can standardize coordination

129
00:07:26,269 --> 00:07:28,359
notes with suitable information rules.

130
00:07:28,809 --> 00:07:32,309
Search-and-rescue teams can organize
mission-preparation summaries.

131
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:36,069
Security teams can structure
patrol briefings, wildland

132
00:07:36,069 --> 00:07:40,090
coordinators can prepare seasonal
orientation forms, and industrial

133
00:07:40,090 --> 00:07:43,399
teams can retain system-training
notes and a public handout.

134
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:48,760
Across those settings, choose fields,
numbering and client access for the task.

135
00:07:49,190 --> 00:07:52,780
Test the actual published form,
verify the saved draft and

136
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:56,739
attachment, and retain the finalized
revision that supports the entry.

